A||N||10||Cozmo||Last  American streetrod?||||5||
Z||000000||Cozmo||10-12-2001||12:36 PM||||Over brews last nite, we were discussing why the Fiero has such a faithful and diverse following.<BR>There were some non-Fiero shoprats hanging around, and in the process of convincing them (no bones were broken, I think <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) we Fieroites (Fieroists?) came up with some observations...<BR>You can put about any engine GM ever made in them.<BR>You can make them look like just about anything you want.<BR>If you build one right, it will run with the world's best supercars.<BR>All this in an "economy commuter" platform...<BR>I think in the future the Fiero will be respected for what it truly is...<BR>The last American streetrod...<BR><P>------------------<BR><B>BobC</B><BR><B>'85 2M6 Targa</B><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> <BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B>||||reg||5
Z||000001||falconhulk||10-12-2001||12:39 PM||||Yes its been called that by a few diffrent mags for years.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||10-12-2001||12:46 PM||||That would be great, and true!  I have found nearly everybody who knows about cars and does not think highly of the Fiero can be convinced otherwise without too much difficulty.  After pointing to some of the many Fiero bodykits available and the 3800 SC, Chevy 350, and N* conversions, they start to quickly realize the potential of this car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||10-12-2001||01:14 PM||||Carroll Shelby rode in mine in 1988, just before I bought it, and drove it back to Michigan. After Carroll road in it he proclaimed it "The Hot Rod Of The Future", and offered to sign the car.  The car proudly displays his signature on the front of the instrument cluster panel (visible thru windshield) and glove box door.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
